# vendor_init property writes.
#
# /vendor/build.prop -- which the build appends device/xsh/k50sv1_64_bsp/vendor.prop
# to (build/make/core/Makefile:489) -- and every setprop in a /vendor rc file are
# evaluated in the vendor_init context, not init:
#   system/core/init/subcontext.cpp:56-58   {"/vendor", kVendorContext="u:r:vendor_init:s0"}
#   system/core/init/property_service.cpp:656-663, 726
#       LoadProperties() picks that context by path prefix and then calls
#       CheckPermissions(), which is a real selinux_check_access() on
#       <type>:property_service:set.
#
# The set_prop(vendor_init, ...) rule(s) below are INERT on this product:
# public/vendor_init.te:193-215 expands here and already grants vendor_init every
# property_type. They are kept for the day PRODUCT_SHIPPING_API_LEVEL is raised
# above 27 -- full explanation at sepolicy/gnss/vendor_init.te.

# vendor.prop: ro.vendor.mtk_pq_color_mode, ro.vendor.mtk_pq_support
# (ro.vendor.mtk_aal_support was a third; it is deleted, unread by anything.)
set_prop(vendor_init, mtk_pq_ro_prop)

# NO "allow vendor_init sysfs_mtk_concurrency_scenario:file ..." -- deleted, not
# forgotten.
#
# rootdir/etc/init/hw/init.mt6755.rc "on post-fs" does hand libbwc's EMI/SMI
# bandwidth node to the media UID:
#   chmod 0664 /sys/bus/platform/drivers/mem_bw_ctrl/concurrency_scenario
#   chown media media /sys/bus/platform/drivers/mem_bw_ctrl/concurrency_scenario
# a /vendor rc does run its chown/chmod in vendor_init, and public/vendor_init.te:29
# does document that chown/chmod needs open+read alongside setattr. The rule that
# followed -- { getattr open read setattr } -- was still dead, because AOSP grants
# all four between two rules of its own:
#   system/sepolicy/public/vendor_init.te:123-133
#     allow vendor_init { fs_type -contextmount_type -keychord_device
#                         -sdcard_type -rootfs -proc_uid_* }:file
#                        { open read setattr map };
#   system/sepolicy/public/vendor_init.te:167
#     allow vendor_init { sysfs_type -sysfs_usermodehelper }:file rw_file_perms;
# rw_file_perms is r_file_perms + w_file_perms (public/global_macros:25,26,29),
# and r_file_perms leads with getattr -- so :167 supplies the getattr that
# :123-133 lacks, and :123-133 supplies the setattr that :167 lacks.
# sysfs_mtk_concurrency_scenario is `fs_type, sysfs_type` (media/file.te:7), so it
# is in both sets.
#
# That second rule is what makes this site different from the proc_mtk_power_*
# nodes in sepolicy/vendor/vendor_init.te, which keep a getattr-only rule: proc
# types have no sysfs_type-style companion grant. Verified the same way -- with
# this rule removed from policy.conf, probes for all four permissions fire under
# checkpolicy, so there is nothing left to grant.
